comdlg32, shell32, winemac: the Mac's open and save panels for the file dialogs

A Windows program that asks for a file or a folder got Wine's own dialog: the Mac's disk as
drive Z:, no Finder sidebar, no external drives by name, no iCloud, no search. It now gets an
NSOpenPanel or NSSavePanel, and the program is handed Windows paths for what was chosen there.

dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_filepanel.m, filepanel.h, macdrv_main.c, unixlib.h, macdrv_cocoa.h
- Three calls into the driver's Unix library: show a panel for a request, ask whether it has
  closed and with what, and forget it (closing it if it is open). The request is one block of
  memory with no pointers in it, and the three parameter structs are laid out the same for a
  32-bit and a 64-bit program, so one function serves both call tables.
- The panel runs modally on the main thread, started from the run loop and not from one of
  the driver's requests: it stays in front of the program's windows, they take no clicks
  meanwhile, and the driver's other work on the main thread goes on, since its request source
  is in the common run loop modes. The Windows thread does not wait inside the driver.
- What a request can say: open or save; files, folders or both; one or several; hidden files;
  no "New Folder"; aliases returned as themselves; the title, the button's text, a line of
  text, the folder to start in, the name to suggest, the default extension; and the file
  types, which become a pop-up under the browser as in the Windows dialog. An open panel
  enables the files the selected type's patterns match ("*.txt;*.log", any wildcard pattern);
  a save panel takes the type's extensions as its allowed content types, so that it adds the
  extension to a name typed without one and asks before replacing the file of that name.
- No panel while a program has captured the displays (it would be behind the game): the call
  fails and Wine's dialog is shown. Above a full-screen window that is only raised over the
  menu bar the panel is shown, at a level above the program's highest window.
- A hidden Edit menu for as long as a panel is open. Wine's menu bar has none, and without
  one the panel's text fields get no Command-V.
- FINEWINE_FILE_PANEL_ANSWER (and _ANSWER_FILTER, _ANSWER_DELAY) give the panel's answer
  ahead, for the tests: "cancel" or Unix paths, one a line. Without a delay no panel is shown.

dlls/comdlg32/macpanel.c, macpanel.h, comdlg32.spec
- __wine_mac_file_panel(): builds the request, has the panel shown, and waits for it while
  handling the thread's window messages, with the owner window disabled as a modal dialog's
  is. The start folder goes over as a Unix path (wine_get_unix_file_name) and the result comes
  back as Unix paths that become Windows ones (wine_get_dos_file_name: a drive letter that
  reaches the folder when the prefix has one, Z: otherwise). The driver's Unix library is
  called by the handle of winemac.drv, so its Windows side needs no export.
- Used when the driver is the Mac one and FINEWINE_NATIVE_FILE_DIALOGS is not 0. It returns
  E_NOTIMPL when there is no panel to be had, and each caller then shows Wine's dialog.
- Exported for shell32, with __wine_mac_file_panel_enabled().

dlls/comdlg32/itemdlg.c (IFileOpenDialog, IFileSaveDialog)
- Show() shows the panel with what the program set: title, button text, file types and the
  selected one, default extension, folder (SetFolder, else SetDefaultFolder, else the folder
  of a file name set with a path), file name, and the options FOS_PICKFOLDERS,
  FOS_ALLOWMULTISELECT, FOS_FORCESHOWHIDDEN, FOS_NODEREFERENCELINKS, FOS_STRICTFILETYPES.
- The choice becomes the dialog's results (GetResult, GetResults), its selection, its folder
  and its file name, and the file type that was selected its index. The program's events get
  OnTypeChange, OnFolderChange and OnFileOk; if OnFileOk refuses, the panel comes back where
  it was. Close() from the program closes the panel and Show() returns what it was given.
- A dialog the program has added controls to (IFileDialogCustomize) or an open button menu
  stays Wine's: a panel has no place for them.

dlls/comdlg32/filedlg.c (GetOpenFileName, GetSaveFileName, A and W)
- The panel gets the title, the filter list (with the custom filter first when there is one),
  the folder and name from lpstrFile or lpstrInitialDir, and the default extension. The result
  is written as the dialog writes it: the path, or for several files the folder and then each
  name; nFileOffset, nFileExtension, lpstrFileTitle, nFilterIndex; OFN_EXTENSIONDIFFERENT;
  OFN_READONLY cleared; the current directory moved unless OFN_NOCHANGEDIR; and for a buffer
  that is too small FALSE with FNERR_BUFFERTOOSMALL and the needed size in the buffer.
- A dialog with a hook or a template of the program's stays Wine's, as does the Windows 3.1
  look.

dlls/shell32/brsfolder.c (SHBrowseForFolder, A and W)
- A folder panel, with the dialog's line of text as the panel's. A callback is served through
  a window that stands in for the dialog: BFFM_SETSELECTION and BFFM_SETOKTEXT sent at
  BFFM_INITIALIZED go to the panel, the choice is shown with BFFM_SELCHANGED, and a callback
  that then disables OK gets the panel again. A root other than the desktop is where the
  panel starts, and a folder outside it is not taken. BIF_BROWSEINCLUDEFILES and
  BIF_NONEWFOLDERBUTTON are followed.
- Browsing for computers or printers, and a root that is not a folder on disk, stay Wine's.

tests/runtime/file-dialogs.c runs all three APIs against given answers, as x64, ARM64 and
32-bit programs: one and several files, a folder, saving, cancelling, the ANSI functions, a
small buffer, a refusing OnFileOk and a refusing folder callback, a path inside the prefix
(C:) and one outside it (Z:), and with a real panel on screen an answer after a delay and
IFileDialog::Close from another thread.

Not tried by hand at the time of writing: choosing in the panel with the mouse, the file
type pop-up, and typing a name into a save panel.

